Tell a redo worker how big the fork is before it rebuilds a page.

A batch names its target blocks up front, and until now that was only
used to keep redo off the bystanders a multi block record references. It
also happens to be the only thing in the process that knows how big a
relation is supposed to be, and nothing was told.

So a worker started every fork at zero blocks, and replaying a record
for block 176526 sent XLogReadBufferExtended down the extension path:
ExtendBufferedRelTo zero fills its way up to the target, through a
buffer pool this process deliberately sets to 1 MB, so every block it
passes is evicted immediately and stored in the page table on the way
out. Serving block N cost N blocks of work and N blocks of memory, once
per batch, and the pool recycles a worker every 64 batches so it began
again from zero.

Measured on server3 (zou #467), a cold sequential scan of a 1.4 GB table
served 8 pages a second with the redo worker at 94 percent of a core,
and a profile of that worker put a third of its cycles in
ExtendBufferedRelShared and another fifth in the hash table underneath
the pages it was storing. It decayed as it went, 35 pages a second early
in the relation and 8 twenty minutes later, which is the shape of a cost
proportional to the block number rather than to the work.

The size is a fiction in this process anyway. Nothing opens a file,
load_page answers an unknown block with zeros, and the only consumer of
zredo_nblocks is postgres deciding whether a block exists. A target
block exists by definition: it is the page the batch was asked to
rebuild.

--- a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_walredo.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_walredo.c
@@ -690,6 +690,7 @@
 handle_target(const uint8 *payload, uint32 len)
 {
 	ZRedoPageKey key;
+	ZRedoFork  *fk;
 	RelFileLocator locator;
 	ForkNumber	fork;
 	BlockNumber block;
@@ -702,6 +703,30 @@
 	key.fork = fork;
 	key.block = block;
 	hash_search(target_tab, &key, HASH_ENTER, NULL);
+
+	/*
+	 * The fork is at least this big, and saying so up front is the whole
+	 * difference between a batch that costs the block it wants and one
+	 * that costs the block number it wants.  Without it a worker that
+	 * starts every fork at zero blocks sends XLogReadBufferExtended down
+	 * the extension path, and ExtendBufferedRelTo zero fills its way up
+	 * to the target through a 1 MB buffer pool, storing every block it
+	 * evicts.  Serving block 176526 then means materializing 176526
+	 * blocks nobody asked about, once per batch, and the pool recycles
+	 * the worker every so often so it starts again from zero.
+	 *
+	 * Lying is free here because the size is already a fiction: nothing
+	 * in this process opens a file, load_page answers an unknown block
+	 * with zeros, and the sole consumer of zredo_nblocks is postgres
+	 * deciding whether the block exists.  A target block exists by
+	 * definition, it is the page the batch was asked to rebuild.
+	 *
+	 * Targets arrive right after the reset, which destroys every smgr
+	 * relation, so no cached relation size can be stale by the time redo
+	 * asks for one.
+	 */
+	fk = fork_entry(locator, fork, true);
+	fk->nblocks = Max(fk->nblocks, block + 1);
 }
 
 static void
